WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.600
Director Ang Lee's Gemini Man has been screened
for critics, and it blew their faces right

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.600
off.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.230
Just when you thought movie magic had reached
its pinnacle, critics are calling it a new

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.880
landmark in visual effects.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.720
Based on the movie's plot about a hired killer
who is stalked by a younger clone of himself,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.910
observers have been quick to draw comparisons
between Gemini Man and another recent flick,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.570
the name of which escapes us.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.699
Just kidding - it's Rian Johnson's 2012 film
Looper, which sports a similar premise.

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:31.210
But Gemini Man has actually been in development
for over 20 years.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.980
The first script for the film was completed
in 1997, and at that time, it was to be directed

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.649
by the late Top Gun director, Tony Scott.

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:43.050
Originally, the idea was to have different
actors play the older and younger versions

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.850
of the main character, like they did in Looper,
but once producers decided to have the same

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.560
actor play both parts, the wait for technology
to catch up began.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:56.780
Well, that technology is finally here, and
director Ang Lee goes hog wild, using cutting

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.510
edge motion capture technology to render the
younger version of Smith.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:07.640
"I play both 50-year-old Henry and 23-year-old
Junior, who is sent to kill him."

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.540
And Lee also shot the film at 120 frames per
second rather than the typical 24 in order

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.570
to give it a sense of visceral realism.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.259
The result, according to virtually every critic
who has been lucky enough to feast their eyes

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:21.430
on the movie, is nothing short of mind-blowing.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:26.330
A common thread among their remarks: this
is the first film in years that you will definitely,

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:29.310
absolutely, positively have to see in 3-D.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:34.939
Engadget editor Devindra Hardawar positively
gushed over the film in a series of tweets.

00:01:34.939 --> 00:01:37.720
"Vibrating from excitement after seeing Gemini
Man.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.590
It uses 120FPS and 3D to create some of the
most exciting action sequences I've seen in

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:44.590
years.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:48.350
Happy to see Ang Lee return to action, the
choreography feels intense because it looks

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:49.350
so realistic."

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.350
He added,

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.360
"The script is a bit generic, but I think
the visuals and Smith's performance overcomes

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.360
it.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.979
In terms of groundbreaking visual experiments,
this feels as important as Avatar."

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:03.590
That's a very bold statement, but other critics
agreed that Gemini Man is arguably the first

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:08.300
film since Avatar that requires you to see
it in 3-D to really get the full experience.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.890
Syfywire feature editor Jordan Zakarin said

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:15.629
"Just saw the final cut of Ang Lee's #GeminiMan
and it's probably the only movie I'd ever

00:02:15.629 --> 00:02:19.090
say absolutely must be seen in 3D (and 4K,
if possible).

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.790
I've never seen anything like it it felt like
I was inside the movie."

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:27.580
The sentiment was echoed by Refinery29 senior
editor Anne Cohen, who wrote,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.840
"Just saw #GeminiMan and it's wiiiiiiild.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.650
The young version of Will Smith is incredible,
plus some truly beautiful action sequences

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.650
and a really great performance by Mary Elizabeth
Winstead.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.180
The only 3D movie I'll ever endorse."

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:45.209
Fandango managing editor Erik Davis agreed
that it needed to be seen in 3-D, and also

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:47.930
highlighted how much detail went into making
the film.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.660
"There's one scene in #GeminiMan where old
Smith and young Smith have an all-out brawl

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:53.660
with one another.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.190
It's pretty amazing to watch.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:03:00.830
I asked Ang Lee how they executed that sequence,
and he said it was so involved it took 9 months

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:01.830
to complete.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.900
9 months for a fight scene!"

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.060
Meanwhile, Yahoo!

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.819
Entertainment senior writer Ethan Alter focused
on the film's groundbreaking use of a super

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:11.280
high frame rate.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.849
"Even high-frame rate skeptics may be converted
by what Ang Lee pulls off in #GeminiMan.

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:22.790
While not seamless, it's probably the strongest
argument for HFR photography I've seen, and

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.830
the illusion actually gets better as the movie
goes along."

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:31.440
The lovefest was perhaps summed up best by
ComingSoon.net senior editor Max Evry:

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.310
"Ang Lee's #GeminiMan is a huge breakthrough
for 120FPS presentation.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.910
Action sequences are breathtaking in this
format.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:44.020
Will Smith's winning performance shows the
level of scrutiny a real movie star can stand

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.020
up to.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:47.590
If you don't see it in theaters you're missing
a true experience."

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:49.420
"Action"

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.180
"Ooh, s---.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.780
That's crazy!!"

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:01.700
All the praise on the film's technical details
is fantastic, but it's Collider's Vinnie Mancuso

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.770
who really cuts to the chase and tells you
what you truly need to know:

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:10.590
"In Gemini Man, the young Will Smith throws
not one, but two (2) motorcycles at the old

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:14.040
Will Smith and that, to me, is very good.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.040
Highly recommend."

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.470
Okay, we're sold.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:20.759
We'll see you in line when Gemini Man hits
the big screen on October 11.

